WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.840
If I use ten times, does the exchange give me 900?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.435
It's not free cash.

00:00:10.435 --> 00:00:14.864
Simply put, 100 of margin controlling a 1,000 position is 10x leverage.

00:00:14.864 --> 00:00:19.480
Price moves act on the position, and your margin takes the profit or loss.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.169
Ignoring fees, a 1% move against you loses about 10 on a 1,000 position,

00:00:40.169 --> 00:00:42.424
and about 20 on a 2,000 position.

00:00:42.424 --> 00:00:46.660
The percentage looks small, but on a small margin it can hurt.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:08.471
Here's an easy mistake.

00:01:08.471 --> 00:01:12.560
If the position size stays the same and you only change the leverage setting,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.533
the P&amp;L in money for the same price move doesn't double.

00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:18.560
What changes is the margin required and your risk buffer.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:40.079
Real liquidation also involves maintenance margin, the mark price and fees.

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:43.814
You can't say 10x always liquidates at exactly a 10% drop.

00:01:43.814 --> 00:01:46.390
Don't use this shortcut for real trades.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:56.760
No.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.430
It magnifies how the position hits your money, not your judgement.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.710
You still have margin left. So why can you be forced out?

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.470
Next: the line you can't see.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.020
Understand first, then act.
